# Hardware notes

### PIONEER DJM-S9
Unlike the old Serato SL1 box (which used a proprietary, non-MIDI USB audio/control stream) (But fortunately was not usb-encoded so we could still tap into the signals for the 4 channels of the turntables output and redirecting them as signal into something such as as MIXXX which I did 10 years back ^_~)


, the Pioneer DJM-S9 is a class-compliant USB MIDI and audio device. When Serato is not running, the DJM-S9 functions as a general-purpose MIDI controller. 

A custom C++ application running on Linux can connect to it via ALSA or JACK MIDI to read messages from the 8 performance pads, buttons, channel faders, and crossfader.Control Capabilities in Generic MIDI ModeCue and Performance Pads: 
 - The 8 multi-color pads send standard MIDI note-on/note-off messages when tapped or held.Faders and Crossfader: The channel volume faders and the magnetic crossfader transmit continuous controller (CC) messages corresponding to their physical positions.
 - Transport & Buttons: Standard buttons on the faceplate (like shift, cue, or parameter triggers) broadcast assignable MIDI codes.
 
 #### Important Technical Nuances
 Not Proprietary, but Class-Compliant: Unlike the SL1, the S9 exposes standard ALSA MIDI ports on Linux without needing specialized user-space driver hacks.Audio Mixing vs. MIDI Data: Moving the physical crossfader or channel faders sends MIDI data to your computer, but it also physically controls the internal analog or digital audio routing passing through the hardware mixer. It behaves simultaneously as a USB audio interface and a MIDI control surface.LED Feedback: Lighting up the performance pads via your C++ application requires sending specific MIDI output note messages back to the mixer's designated MIDI channels.

